A||||7||Alex2004||Car Lost all power driving down hiway(ignition,most electrical etc)!!!!||||8||
Z||000000||Alex2004||11-30-2001||11:50 PM||||WTF happen?  I was driving home from watching my school play b-ball and my car suddenly just dies, headlights get dim, bright, then dim and the car shuts off...and i coast until i can pull over to a safe place.  Headlights/parking lights work, flashers also work, so does the dome light but i have no ignition power, i tried to start the car...nothing happen...none of the instrument lights come on except flashers also...NO check engine, seat belt, anything.  I have an 84 SE.  I checked all of the fuses, none are blown.  What happened.  I'm worried right now because my car is 30 miles from home stranded on a hiway.  Also my alarm isn't working now either. power locks and windows also quit working but whenever i hit the lock button i hear a click under passenger side dash.  PLEASE LET ME KNOW SOON!!!   Thanks...<P>Alex...||||reg||8
Z||000001||fieroparts.com||12-01-2001||12:37 AM||||sounds like one of the inline fuses!||||reg||14
Z||000002||rogergarrison||12-01-2001||06:23 AM||||ya, sounds like a fusible link, prob the one by the battery fried. Just use a circuit tester probe to see if the power is getting thru it. Maybe you battery shorted out too. Ive seen them short internally when there older.<P>IF YOU HAVENT GOTTEN IT OFF THE ROAD YET, PAY A TOW TRUCK THE $50 AS SOON AS POSSIBLE, VANDLES WILL TOTAL IT SITTING BY THE ROADSIDE! IF THEY HAVENT ALREADY. It will def cost more to fix than the $50.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||MickeyAsh||12-01-2001||09:20 AM||||This is an extreme case mind you, but will give you some things to check... When that happened to me I was on a major interstate in rush hour traffic, apparently some idiot routed my positive battery cable right next to the hinge for my decklid... it wore through, shorted and fried two of my ground cables, then the power cable proceded to weld itself into my decklid henge, and my battery cracked. Again, just a few things to check... all the ground cables and of course your battery connections and cables. My .02...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodrv6||12-01-2001||10:07 AM||||The 84's have a couple of fusible links attatched to the starter solonoid terminal.  The heat from the exhaust tends to make them brittle and easily broken.  It sounds like one of them has come apart.  If both of them break the whole car goes dead!  Happened to me one night.  The 85's and newer rerouted the wire harness and it's not as much of a problem.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000005||sqoach||12-01-2001||10:50 AM||||Exact same thing happened to me, but I was in town. I was cruising around town, and my car was acting funny. 'Bout another half mile, it just died. Lights still worked, but I couldn't start it. Nothing turned on. I went home and checked the manual, and found that a bad fusible link would cause this. I checked it out, and the fusible link wire had broken in half  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> Not sure how it happened. Looked like it just rotted through. Anyway, got that fixed and solved the problem.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>                <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigsqoach2.gif"> <BR><B>86 SE V6 - daily driver</B><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 Sport Coupe 2.5L 5spd (soon to be sold)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex2004||12-01-2001||10:54 AM||||Ok, well today we went and looked at my car and it was the one of the starter wires burnt through,the fuseable link...I wonder what causes it.  I was running my fog lights and they are off ignition power...and the do NOT have a relay because i have not bought one yet.  Could that have caused it?  Or just old age and heat?  Well thanks for you help!!! It's safe and at home now...no vandals got to it!....Thanks Again!!!<P>Alex...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierowrecker||12-01-2001||11:39 AM||||Hey Alex!<BR>My 84 used to toast fusable links ocasionally, till I decided to look for the cause...<BR>The alternator/battery wire (runs from the alternator to the starter solenoid battery terminal), is spliced and is dressed along the pulley end of the engine behind a plastic cover...<BR>The splice was taped over, but the tape unwrapped, alowing the splice to short against the block, burning out the link at the starter...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
